 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Tempura with plain rice is mild; a craggy karaage crust stays crunchy next to the rice and takes the spicy mayo better.</li><li>Same double-fry idea as the <a href=\"karaage.html\">karaage</a> page, on shrimp.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why karaage, not tempura"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Tails hanging out look like classic tenmusu but make a mess to eat.</li><li>Curl the peeled shrimp so the whole piece sits inside.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Tails off, curled"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The spicy mayo is the seasoning — without it the rice is only salted from your hands.</li><li>Don’t drown the shrimp or the rice won’t close.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Sauce inside"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Not eating them right away?</li><li>Keep the nori strips in an airtight bag and wrap them on just before eating.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Crisp nori"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Never fill the pot more than half full; dry the shrimp of excess marinade before coating.</p>",
   "title_html": "Oil safety"
  }
 ],
